```c
#include <stdio.h>
void f(void) {
    static int c = 0;
    int a = 0;
    c++; a++;
    printf("%d %d  ", c, a);
}
int main(void) { f(); f(); f(); return 0; }
```

`a` is created fresh on every call, incremented to 1, and destroyed. `c` is created once, before `main` runs, and survives between calls.

Output: `1 1  2 1  3 1`

The initialiser `static int c = 0;` executes **once**, not on every call — that is the whole point of the example.

