```cpp
class A { public: A(){cout<<"A";} ~A(){cout<<"a";} };
class B { public: B(){cout<<"B";} ~B(){cout<<"b";} };
class D : public A {
    B m;
  public:
    D(){cout<<"D";} ~D(){cout<<"d";}
};
int main(){ D obj; }
```

Construction: base `A` → member `m` of type `B` → body of `D`. That prints `ABD`.

Destruction is the mirror image: body of `~D` → member `m` → base `A`. That prints `dbа`, i.e. `dba`.

Full output: **ABDdba**.

The pattern to remember is that the output is a palindrome-like sandwich: whatever was built first is torn down last.

## Copy constructors and the shallow copy problem

A copy constructor has the signature `T(const T&)`. It runs when an object is initialised from another, passed by value, or returned by value.

If you do not write one, the compiler generates a **member-wise (shallow)** copy. For a class holding a raw pointer that is a bug: two objects end up owning the same buffer, and the second destructor frees memory the first already freed.

```cpp
class Str {
    char *p;
  public:
    Str(const char *s){ p = new char[strlen(s)+1]; strcpy(p,s); }
    Str(const Str &o){ p = new char[strlen(o.p)+1]; strcpy(p,o.p); }  // deep
    ~Str(){ delete[] p; }
};
```

Without that second constructor the class double-frees. The rule of thumb — if you need a destructor, you almost certainly need a copy constructor and an assignment operator too.

Note the parameter must be a **reference**. Passing by value would require a copy, which would call the copy constructor, which would require a copy — infinite recursion, so the compiler rejects it.
